wolly (0.2.0) stable; urgency=low

  * An OpenID provider for a fixed set of first-party clients, Wolly.Op
    with its parts (OpBase, OpClient, OpGrant, OpKey, OpRandom, OpStore,
    OpToken, OpUrl): discovery, the key set, authorization with a code
    challenge (S256 only), the token endpoint with refresh tokens in
    families and client credentials, logout, ID tokens signed with
    ML-DSA-44 or the composite ML-DSA-44-Ed25519, in a task of its own
    with 1 MiB of stack. The tables match those of an earlier provider
    written in Go, column for column.

  * Around it: accounts that sign in with an e-mail address, a password
    and a second factor (Wolly.Account), a delay per key after failed
    attempts and failures counted in a window to ban (Wolly.Backoff,
    Wolly.Strikes), a proof of work in front of an expensive step
    (Wolly.Pow), values sealed in the Fernet form that Go implementations
    write (Wolly.Fernet), and an HTTP server for development without a web
    server in front (Wolly.DevServer). TOTP takes the hash of a code as a
    choice; a passkey login may name nobody, and a user registers one
    passkey at a time.

  * Every programme answers version --full with the C libraries it links
    and info with its system report; the example servers and the tools
    qruri, buntipng, view and acme run in a sandbox (pledge and unveil,
    Landlock and seccomp) and print it on sandbox or -s.

  * Searches of the log store: a search takes no write lock; host: uses
    an index on the lower-case host and the time; an equal term under and
    filters the set of its sibling; a word in a window of at most 100 000
    lines reads only the hits of that window (an hour of a million lines
    in 2.5 ms instead of 244 ms).

  * Wolly.Account reads its two flags as integers on PostgreSQL as well.

  * scripts/test.sh also runs hilde test --contracts over each module
    with a contract.

 -- Heiko Zimmermann <code@heiko-zimmermann.com>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0100

wolly (0.1.0) stable; urgency=low

  * First release. Wolly is a web framework in Hilde: an application is a
    FastCGI responder on a Unix socket behind Hiawatha, on the server loop
    of Hilde's Standard.FcgiServer. 63 modules under src/Wolly, each with
    the contracts of its public functions or the reason why not.

  * Requests and answers: the life of the process with SIGTERM and SIGINT,
    request with decoded query and cookies, answer with checked headers,
    a router of method and pattern with 404, 405 and 400, steps before and
    after the router, security headers and one access-log line with a
    request id per request.

  * Sign-in and protection: users with Argon2id hashes, sessions rotated at
    login, throttling per client and per name, the __Host-wolly cookie, an
    audit log, roles, request forgery guards of form token and origin, a
    form guard with honeypot and time window, bans, limits, bearer tokens,
    passkeys, TOTP as second factor and a client of RAMBO (OpenID Connect
    with PKCE, ID tokens checked against ML-DSA keys).

  * Data: SQLite and PostgreSQL behind one dialect, entities and edges,
    a cache, metrics, a log store with full-text and sound index and its
    query, sealed secrets, pseudonyms and a vault for the keys of a server.

  * Pages: views with templates of the tenant read at run time and searched
    in linear time, Markdown, Atom feeds, charts, code highlighted, a
    dashboard, QR and Bunti codes, GIF, JPEG and WebP read in Hilde, and
    asset versions from the SHA-256 of the content.

  * Programmes under cli/ (acme, buntipng, qruri, sshrun, vault, view) and
    the examples hello, login and contact; each example is tested behind
    its own Hiawatha over HTTP and TLS. Each programme prints its version
    line on version, -V or --version; the release number comes from
    Wolly.Version, which scripts/check-version.pl holds equal to this file.

  * Built and tested in a container image on Linux (arm64 and amd64), and
    on OpenBSD 8.0.

 -- Heiko Zimmermann <code@heiko-zimmermann.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0100
